### GATEWAY  ·  everything else is proxied; these two routes are its own.
###
### GET /books/:id/page composes a book with its reviews. The dependencies are
### NOT symmetric:
###   book-service    REQUIRED — no book, no page
###   review-service  OPTIONAL — renders without it, marked "unavailable"
###                              (which is not the same as none)

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@email = amr@example.com
@password = password123


### Health  →  200
GET {{baseUrl}}/health


### Log in + a book with a review, so the page has something to compose
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "email": "{{email}}", "password": "{{password}}" }

@accessToken = {{login.response.body.data.tokens.accessToken}}

###
# @name pageBook
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: {{$guid}}

{ "title": "Composed Page Fixture", "description": "Has at least one review.", "genre": ["test"] }

@bookId = {{pageBook.response.body.data.id}}

###
POST {{baseUrl}}/reviews
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "bookId": "{{bookId}}", "rating": 5, "comment": "Composed." }


### The composed page  →  200, book + reviews in one response.
### The gateway asks for a fixed number of reviews, so one page view no longer
### costs whatever the most-reviewed book happens to hold.
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookId}}/page


### Degraded: `docker stop review-service`, resend the request above, then
### start it again. Expect 200 with reviews "unavailable" — NOT an empty array,
### which would assert the book has no reviews.


### Missing book  →  404, because book-service is the required half
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/page
